.TH \fBpetrify 1cad
.SH NAME
\fBpetrify\fR	- synthesize Petri nets and asynchronous controllers
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpetrify\fR [\fIoptions\fR]* [\fIinfile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBPetrify\fP is a tool for the synthesis of bounded Petri nets and 
logic synthesis of asynchronous controllers.

\fBPetrify\fP initially performs a token flow analysis of the Petri net and
produces a finite transition system (TS). In the initial TS, all transitions
with the same label are considered as one event. The TS is then transformed 
and transitions relabeled to fulfil the conditions required to
obtain a Petri net with bisimilar or trace-equivalent behavior.
Some properties for the synthesized Petri net can be imposed (e.g.
free-choice, unique-choice, pure, state-machine decomposable, etc.).

Additionally, \fBpetrify\fP can interpret the Petri net as a Signal
Transition Graph (STG), in which events represent rising/falling
transitions of digital signals. From an STG, \fBpetrify\fP can
synthesize a speed-independent circuit by solving the problems
of state encoding, logic synthesis, logic decomposition and technology
mapping onto a gate library. \fBPetrify\fP can also synthesize circuit
under timing assumptions specified by the designer or automatically
generated by the tool.

\fBPetrify\fP reads the input description from \fIstdin\fP and writes
the resulting STG to \fIstdout\fP unless otherwise specified.

.SH GENERAL OPTIONS
.TP 0.5i
\fB-h\fP
Help mode, print the usage.
.TP
\fB-v\fP
Print version only.
.TP
\fB-d\fP[\fIn\fP]
Debug mode. Prints runtime information. The maximum level of debugging
is \fB-d2\fP. \fB-d\fP and \fB-d1\fP are equivalent.
.TP
\fB-o\fP \fIoutfile\fP
Write the resulting STG to \fIoutfile\fP. Otherwise, the result
is written to \fIstdout\fP.
.TP
\fB-no\fP
Output Petri net is not generated.
.TP
\fB-err\fP \fIerrfile\fP
Verbose information and errors written into \fIerrfile\fP 
(instead of \fIstderr\fP).
.TP
\fB-ip\fP
Implied (1-fanin 1-fanout) places are explicitly written in the output
description. If this option is not used, implied places are described as
transition-transition arcs.
.TP
\fB-dead\fP
Do not check for the existence of deadlock states (no events enabled).
If this option is not used, an error message is given and no synthesis
is performed when the specification has deadlock states.
.TP
\fB-sis\fP
Write the output file in SIS compatible style. Currently this means that
toggle transitions are written with the ~ suffix.

.SH OPTIONS FOR PETRI NET SYNTHESIS
.TP 0.5i
\fB-all\fP
All minimal pre-regions are generated. If this option is not specified,
only a subset of minimal regions sufficient to synthesize a Petri net
are generated.
The option \fB-all\fP is computationally more
expensive, although it may allow to find optimal irredundant nets.
.TP
\fB-sat\fP
Generates a \fIminimal saturated\fP (possibly place-redundant) Petri net.
This option requires the generation of all minimal regions.
If this option is not used, a
place-irredundant Petri net is obtained (a Petri net is place-irredundant 
when none of its places can be removed without changing its behavior).
.TP
\fB-min\fP
The places of the resulting Petri net correspond to minimal regions. If this
option is not specified, minimal regions can be merged and, thus, a smaller
place count obtained. The option \fB-sat\fP overrides \fB-min\fP.
.TP
\fB-opt\fP
When this option is specified, \fBpetrify\fP attempts to find the best
possible result. Using this option may be computationally very expensive
for large nets. It is equivalent to \fB-all\fP
\fB-s\fP \fB-rc\fP and it overrides the option \fB-sat\fP.
.TP
\fB-hide\fP \fIsignal_list\fP
The events corresponding to the signals in the list are hidden before
synthesis starts. \fIsignal_list\fP is a list of signals separated by
commas. Eventually, this list may specify groups of signals using
the keywords \fI.inputs\fP, \fI.outputs\fP, etc. For example, 
\fB-hide\fP \fIa,.dummy\fP.
.TP
\fB-bisim\fP
Maintain bisimilarity when minimizing the transition system. In case
internal and/or dummy events are hidden, weak bisimilarity is preserved.
If not specified, minimization only preserves trace equivalence.
.TP
\fB-mints\fP
Minimize the transition system before synthesis. If such option is
not specified, only direct ascendant/descendant states through silent
events are merged when they are equivalent (silent events are removable
events specified by the option \fB-hide\fP).
Minimizing the transition system is computationally more expensive and
may preclude excitation closure in case there are non-confluent states.

.SH OPTIONS FOR PETRI NET PROPERTIES
.TP 0.5i
\fB-p\fP
Produce a pure Petri net (no self-loop places).
.TP
\fB-fc\fP 
Produce a Free-Choice Petri net.
.TP
\fB-efc\fP 
Produce an Extended Free-Choice Petri net.
.TP
\fB-uc\fP 
Produce a Unique-Choice Petri net (with no extended free choices).
.TP
\fB-euc\fP 
Produce a Unique-Choice Petri net (with extended free choices).
.TP
\fB-er\fP
A different transition (label) is generated for each excitation region.
If this option is not used, \fBpetrify\fP attempts to use as few labels
as possible for each event of the transition system. This option may produce
structurally nicer, although larger, Petri nets. It also helps to reduce
the complexity of the label splitting algorithm.
.TP
\fB-sm\fP 
Produce a state-machine-decomposable Petri net.

.SH OPTIONS FOR STG TRANSFORMATIONS
.TP 0.5i
\fB-tog\fP
All signal transitions are converted to toggle transitions.
.TP
\fB-untog\fP
All signal transitions are converted to positive and/or negative
transitions. This may imply to unfold the original transition system.
.TP
\fB-2ph\fP
Expand channel events to 2-phase protocol. This is the default option when
synthesis of an asynchronous circuit must be done.
.TP
\fB-4ph\fP
Expand channel events to 4-phase protocol. The return-to-zero events are
inserted by maintaining the maximum concurrency with the rest of events.
.TP
\fB-redc\fP
Reduce concurrency to improve the quality of the resulting circuit.
This option can be combined with the statements \fB.slowenv\fP,
\fB.slow\fP, \fB.concurrent\fP, \fB.ordered\fP and \fB.late\fP
to indicate which events are assumed to
be slow. Concurrency between slow events is always preserved.
.TP
\fB-redz\fP
Reduce only the concurrency of return-to-zero events. The concrrency
of the rest of events is preserved.

.SH OPTIONS FOR SYNTHESIS OF ASYNCHRONOUS CIRCUITS
.TP 0.5i
\fB-csc\fP[\fIn\fP]
Check and force Complete State Coding (CSC). \fBPetrify\fP exits with non-zero
status when CSC cannot be completely solved. \fIn\fP is an optional
parameter used during the exploration of blocks to partition the set of
states for the insertion of new signals. It indicates that blocks
of up to \fIn\fP intersecting concurrent regions will be generated to
explore the insertion of signals. If \fIn\fP is not specified, the value
\fIn=1\fP (no intersection) is assumed. Increasing \fIn\fP may result in a
better exploration of candidates at the expense of more computational cost.
.TP
\fB-icsc\fP[\fIn\fP]
Similar as the option \fB-csc\fP[\fIn\fP]. Moreover, when CSC has been solved
by inserting more than one signal, \fBpetrify\fP attempts to hide some
of them and solve CSC again to improve the final solution. Any signal
declared as \fIinternal\fP is eligible to be hidden in this phase.
.TP
\fB-tcsc\fP
State signals to solve CSC are inserted aiming at improving 
the circuit's performance. This is achieved by increasing the concurrency
of those events that appear in critical paths. This option is the default
when the \fB-topt\fP or \fB-atopt\fP options are used.
.TP
\fB-acsc\fP
State signals to solve CSC are inserted aiming at improving 
the circuit's area. This option is the default
when the \fB-topt\fP and \fB-atopt\fP options are not used.
.TP
\fB-io
Preserve I/O interface. This option prevents the creation
of new trigger dependencies to input events when inserting new signals 
or reducing concurrency. When not used, new trigger dependencies to
slow input events are allowed.
This option is ignored when timing assumptions are
used for logic synthesis (options \fB-topt\fP or \fB-atopt\fP).
.TP
\fB-avg\fP
This option is only applicable when CSC is to be solved and timing optimization 
is enabled (see \fB-tcsc\fP). When inserting new CSC signals,
\fBpetrify\fP will attempt to improve the average response time of the circuit
by increasing the concurrency of the new state signals. Timing assumptions
will be reported when the environment is assumed to be slow enough to allow
the state signals stabilize before new input events occur. These assumptions
will only be generated for those input events declared as \fI slow\fP.
When this option is not specified and timing optimization is enabled, 
\fPpetrify\fB will attempt to improve the worst-case response time.
.TP
\fB-cg\fP
Generate a complex-gate implementation of the circuit (one complex
gate for each non-input signal). If neither \fB-cg\fP nor \fB-gc\fP 
are specified, logic decomposition is performed until all functions are 
mappable onto library gates (if the option \fB-tm\fP is specified) and their 
literal count and support do not exceed the maximum allowed 
(see options \fB-lit\fP\fIn\fP and \fB-lut\fP\fIn\fP).
.TP
\fB-gc\fP
Generate an implementation of the circuit with generalized C elements.
If neither \fB-cg\fP nor \fB-gc\fP 
are specified, logic decomposition is performed until all functions are 
mappable onto library gates (if the option \fB-tm\fP is specified) and their 
literal count and support do not exceed the maximum allowed 
(see options \fB-lit\fP\fIn\fP and \fB-lut\fP\fIn\fP).
.TP
\fB-mc\fP
Generate an implementation based on monotonic covers (only C elements
are used as asynchronous latches). No technology mapping is done when
this option is used.
.TP
\fB-nosi\fP
Generate complex gates even if the specification of the circuit is
not speed-independent.
.TP
\fB-tm\fP[\fIn\fP]
Perform technology mapping onto a given library of gates. See the option
\fB-lib\fP for information on how to specify the gate library. The optional
parameter \fIn\fP is an integer specifying the depth of search to collapse
gates for boolean matching. The default value is \fIn=2\fP. Higher values
perform a more exhaustive search.
.TP
\fB-tm_ratio\fP[\fIx\fP]
Specifies how the mapper must trade off delay and area. \fIx\fP is a real
value between 0 and 1. The default value \fIx=0\fP seeks for a minimum area
circuit, whereas \fIx=1\fP seeks for minimum delay. 
.TP
\fB-log\fP \fIlogfile\fP
Generate a file with exhaustive information about logic synthesis, 
timing assumptions, etc.
This file is crucial for the option \fI-selckt\fP that enables the designer
to interactively select the preferred gate implementation of each non-input
signal. If this option is not specified, the default log file is 
\fIpetrify.log\fP.
The file name \fB-\fP can be used to redirect to the standard output.
.TP
\fB-nolog\fP
Do not generate the log file.
.TP
\fB-selckt\fP
Initiates an interactive selection of the implementation of each non-input
signal. The information about the eligible implementations is reported
in the log file (see option \fI-log\fP).
.TP
\fB-eqn\fP \fIeqnfile\fP
Generate a logic description of the circuit in EQN format and write into the 
file \fIeqnfile\fP. 
The file name \fB-\fP can be used to redirect to the standard output.
.TP
\fB-blif\fP \fIbliffile\fP
Generate a logic description of the circuit in BLIF format and write into the 
file \fIbliffile\fP.
The file name \fB-\fP can be used to redirect to the standard output.
.TP
\fB-vl\fP \fIvlogfile\fP
Generate a logic description of the circuit in Verilog and write into the 
file \fIvlogfile\fP.
The file name \fB-\fP can be used to redirect to the standard output.
.TP
\fB-rst0\fP
Generate a netlist with a reset pin active at low. This option only applies
for Verilog netlits.
.TP
\fB-rst1\fP
Similar to \fB-rst0\fP but with the pin active at high.
.TP
\fB-lib\fP \fIlibfile\fP
When a library gate is required, the file \fIlibfile\fP (in \fIgenlib\fP
format) is read. In case it is not found, the environment
variable \fIPETRIFY_LIB_PATH\fP is used as the path to find the file.
If the option \fB-lib\fP is not used, the file name \fIpetrify.lib\fP
is used by default. In case more than one \fB-lib\fP option is specified,
all the library files are read and appended to the same gate library.
\fBPetrify\fP uses all combinational gates and
asynchronous latches found in the library unless the option \fB-latch\fP
is specified. It is the user's responsability
to guarantee that all the gates in the library can be safely used for
asynchronous circuit implementation (e.g. they are hazard free).
.TP
\fB-latch\fP \fIstr\fP
Specify a restricted set of latches to be used for synthesis. \fIstr\fP
can contain any string of characters from the set \fIcdrs\fP (capital
letters are also allowed), which respectively correspond to the following
latches: Muller C element, D latch, reset-dominant SR latch and set-dominant
SR latch. These latches are only used if found in the library. In case this
option is not specified, any asynchronous latch in the library is used.
.TP
\fB-nolatch\fP
No latches are used for logic synthesis and technology mapping.
.TP
\fB-lit\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the maximum number of literals (in factored form) allowed for each 
combinational function. Logic decomposition of combinational gates is
attempted when they cannot be mapped onto any library gate or when their
literal count exceeds \fIn\fP (even if they are mappable onto a gate).
If not specified, logic decomposition is attempted until all gates are
mappable onto library gates. If \fIn\fP is not specified, the value
\fIn=2\fP is assumed.
.TP
\fB-lut\fP[\fIn\fP]
Similarly to the option \fB-lit\fP, but specifying the maximum support
of the combinational function. This option is intended to be used for
LUT-based synthesis, where \fIn\fP is the number of inputs of each
lookup table.
If \fIn\fP is not specified, the value \fIn=4\fP is assumed.
.TP
\fB-topt\fP
Timing optimization is applied. The statements in the specification related
to timing assumptions are taken into account. If CSC is to be solved, 
\fBpetrify\fP to take advantage of the events declared as \fIslow\fP to
improve the performance (response time) of the circuit at the expense
of a possible increase of logic complexity.
.TP
\fB-atopt\fP
Similar to \fB-topt\fP. Additionally, \fBpetrify\fP automatically generates
timing assumptions for performance optimization. 

.SH ADVANCED OPTIONS (only for expert users)

.TP
\fBFor Petri net synthesis:\fP
.sp
.TP 0.5i
\fB-s\fP[\fIn\fP]
Defines the search depth to find good regions for label splitting. If
\fIn\fP is large, the search may become computationally expensive. The
default value is \fB-s3\fP. The search is never pruned if the option \fB-s\fP 
(\fIn\fP not specified) is used.
.TP
\fB-rc\fP[\fIn\fP]
Maximum number of non-essential places to obtain a minimal irredundant
net. If \fIn\fP is not specified, an optimal solution is found, although
it may be computionally very expensive if the number of non-essential
places is large. The default is \fIn\fP=40.
The current implementation
eliminates non-essential places greedily until \fIn\fP 
places are left. Next, an optimal solution is found for the rest of
places. The cost of each region is calculated as fi+fo+1, where fi and
fo correspond to the number of fanin and fanout arcs respectively.
With n=0, the elimination is completely greedy (and faster).
.TP
\fB-cl\fP[\fIn\fP]
Maximum size of the compatibility graph built to find a one-hot -> log
reencoding. If \fIn\fP is not specified, no size limit is assumed.
If \fIn\fP<3, reencoding is not done. The default value is n=31.
This option controls the number of BDD variables used when trying to
find a good encoding for the transition system.
.sp
.TP -0.5i
\fBFor state encoding:\fP
.sp
.TP 0.5i
\fB-ncsc\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the maximum number of signals to be inserted for solving CSC. If
\fIn\fP is too small, CSC may not be solved. If \fIn\fP is not specified,
only one signal is inserted.
.TP
\fB-nice_csc\fP
Try to insert CSC signals with simple causality relations with their
neighbouring events. This option may derive nicer solutions at the expense
of more area or less performance.
.TP
\fB-seq\fP
Do not try to increase the concurrency of the new state signals inserted
to solve CSC.
.TP
\fB-fr\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the frontier width for the exploration of blocks for
state signal insertion. The default is \fB-fr\fP\fI1\fP.
A very wide frontier is used when \fIn\fP is not specified.
.TP
\fB-redfr\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the frontier width for the exploration of concurrency
reduction. The default is \fB-redfr\fP\fI3\fP.
A very wide frontier is used when \fIn\fP is not specified.
.TP
\fB-cw\fP[\fIx\fP]
Specify a factor (float between 0 and 1) to tune the 
eagerness of the signal insertion algorithm to solve CSC conflicts.
Small values of \fIx\fP give priority to solving CSC conflicts rather
than optimizing logic. The default value for \fIx\fP (0.2) has proved to be
appropriate for a large set of benchmarks. If \fIx\fP is not specified,
the search is guided towards solving the maximum number of CSC conflicts
for each inserted signal.
.TP
\fB-trcsc\fP
Report a set of traces that lead to states with CSC conflicts. A trace
is generated for each state with a CSC conflict that has no predecessor
states with CSC conflicts. The traces are reported in the logfile.
.sp
.TP -0.5i
\fBFor logic synthesis and decomposition:\fP
.sp
.TP 0.5i
\fB-boolmin\fP[\fIn\fP]
Define the type of boolean minimizer used by \fBpetrify\fP.
The values \fIn=0...4\fP select different types of BDD-based boolean
minimizers trading-off efficiency and optimality. Low values of \fIn\fP
select more efficient but less accurate minimizers. For \fIn=0\fP,
the minimizer only generates one irredundant cover each time it is called.
For \fIn=1...3\fP, several irredundant covers may eventually be generated.
The value \fIn=4\fP selects espresso as boolean minimizer.
The default value is \fIn=2\fP, which is a good trade-off between efficiency 
and optimality.
.TP
\fB-gcmodel\fP \fIr:w:i:e:l\fP
Specify the parameters for the delay estimation of the gates generated
by either the \fB-cg\fP or \fB-gc\fP options. The five parameters must
be positive real numbers. If one of the parameters is not specified,
the default value is used. \fIr\fP is the mobility ratio between n and p 
devices (default: 2.5), \fIw\fP is the width of p devices assuming that
n devices are 1 unit wide (default: 1.5), \fIi\fP is the internal capacitance
per width unit of each device diffusion (default: 0.25), \fIe\fP is the
extra capacitance for connecting the pull-up and pull-down networks
(default: 1.0) and \fIl\fP is the load capacitance of the gate (default: 3.0).
The unit for \fIi\fP and \fIe\fP is gate capacitance. The unit for \fIl\fP
is the input capacitance of one inverter (the default value of 3.0 indicates
that the gate has a fanout of 3 inverters). Example of usage: 
\fB-gcmodel\fP \fI:2::0.5:4\fP indicating \fIw=2.0\fP, \fIe=0.5\fP and
\fIl=4.0\fP.
.TP
\fB-gcm\fP
Same as \fB-gc\fP but generating monotonic covers for the pull-up and pull-down
networks of the generalized C element. This option may be useful if
the networks of the generalized C element must be implemented as independent
gates and still speed independence is to be preserved.
.TP
\fB-nmap\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the maximum number of signals to be inserted for technology mapping.
If \fIn\fP is not specified, only one signal is inserted.
.TP
\fB-cgd\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the maximum number of complex gates generated for each non-input
signal. These complex gates are the functions used to explore algebraic
decompositions.
The default value is \fIn=3\fP. 
If \fIn\fP is not specified, all irredundant covers generated by the
boolean minimizer are used for decomposition.
.TP
\fB-algd\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the maximum number of algebraic decompositions explored for each
combinational gate during logic decomposition. The default value is
\fIn=30\fP. If \fIn\fP is not specified, an exhaustive search of algebraic
decompositions is performed.
.TP
\fB-mcd\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the maximum number of monotonous covers explored for each positive
and negative excitation region of a signal.
The default value is \fIn=4\fP.
If \fIn\fP is not specified, an exhaustive search of monotonous
covers is performed.
.TP
\fB-boold\fP[\fIn\fP]
Specify the maximum number of boolean decompositions explored for each 
latch and 2-input gate (AND, OR). The default value is \fIn=3\fP.
Increasing \fIn\fP may result in a significant increase of computational
cost.
If \fIn\fP is not specified, an exhaustive search of boolean
decompositions is performed.
.TP
\fB-ed\fP
Perform an exhaustive exploration of decompositions for each boolean
function. This option overrides the options \fB-cgd\fP,
\fB-algd\fP, \fB-mcd\fP and
\fB-boold\fP and may become computationally expensive even for small
circuits.
.TP -0.5i
\fBFor BDD operations:\fP
.sp
.TP 0.5i
\fB-trflat\fP[\fIn\fP]
Define the maximum BDD node limit when flattening a partitioned 
transition relation into a single BDD. Flattening stops when no more
BDDs can be merged without exceeding the node limit. The default node limit
is 2000. If \fIn\fP is not specified, no limit is assumed (i.e. transition
relations are represented monolithically).
.TP
\fB-notog\fP
Do not use toggle changes in transitions relations. If not specified, the
representation of transition relations is optimized by means of a special
encoding for toggling variables.
.TP
\fB-noreord\fP
Do not reorder BDD variables dynamically.
.TP
\fB-reord\fP[\fIn\fP]
BDD variable reordering frequency for traversal. Reordering is done
once out of \fIn\fP iterations.



.SH INPUT SYNTAX
\fBPetrify\fP reads and writes descriptions in \fIastg\fP format (used by SIS).
Furthermore, several enhancements have been introduced to allow the
specification of channels, unsafe nets, level transitions,
boolean guards, weighted arcs and inhibitor arcs. The
differences from the \fIastg\fP format are illustrated below by means of 
examples.
.sp
\fBWeighted and inhibitor arcs:\fP
.sp
\a.graph
.br
a+ p1(2) b+ p2
.br
p2 d-(0)
.sp
indicates that the event \fIa+\fP has arcs to
.sp
.in +0.4i
.ti -0.2i
- 
place \fIp1\fP with weight 2
.ti -0.2i
- 
transition \fIb+\fP with weight 1 (implicit places cannot have weighted arcs)
.ti -0.2i
- 
place \fIp2\fP with weight 1 (default)
.in -0.4i
.sp
and that place \fIp2\fP has an inhibitor arc (weight 0) to transition \fId-\fP

.sp
\fBToggle transitions:\fP
.sp
The suffix ~ is not required to specify toggle transitions. Thus, "s"
and "s~" are the same transition (similarly for "s/1" and "s~/1").
.sp
\fBDon't care transitions:\fP
.sp
A transition \fIa*\fP indicates that signal \fIa\fP may or may not change
value, i.e. it behaves non-deterministically as a toggle or a dummy
transition. The designer must be careful when using this type of transitions
that can easily derive inconsistently encoded specifications.
.sp
\fBLevel transitions:\fP
.sp
A transition \fIa^0\fP indicates signal \fIa\fP will be go to level 0
without necessarily making a transition. It will behave as a dummy
transition in case signal \fIa\fP is already at level 0. Similarly,
transition \fIa^1\fP indicates signal \fIa\fP going to level 1.
.sp
\fBBoolean guards on transitions:\fP
.sp
Each instance of a transition can be annotated with a boolean guard.
For example, the following line of the graph
.sp
p0 a+ ? b*!c d- ? !b e+
.br
.sp
indicates that place \fIp0\fP is a predecessor of transitions
\fIa+\fP, \fId-\fP and \fIe+\fP. Transition \fIa+\fP will be
only fired when the guard \fIb*!c\fP holds (i.e. \fIb=1\fP
and \fIc=0\fP).
Transition \fId-\fP will be fired
when \fIb=0\fP, whereas no guard is specified for \fIe+\fP.
For implementation reasons, any signal supporting a boolean
guard must be a signal of the STG and
declared in the \fI.initial state\fP statement
with its initial value. The behavior of these signals
must be specified in the STG. Boolean guards can only be expressed
as a conjuntion of literals. 
.sp
In case more that one guard is specified for one transition,
for example,
.sp
p0 a+ ? b*c
.br
a+ ? !c d+
.br
.sp
the conjunction of all the guards will be considered.
In this example, transition \fIa+\fP will never be fired
since \fIb*c*!c=0\fP.
.sp
Weighted arcs and boolean guards can be combined as
shown in the following example:
.sp
p0 a+(3) ? b
.br
.sp
\fBSignals with free return-to-zero:\fP
.sp
It is posible to specify signals in which only the positive or negative
transitions are meaningful, whereas the complementary transitions are
only required to maintain the consistency of the encoding. This is declared
as follows:
.sp
\a.inputs a+ b c-
.br
.sp
(similar declarations can be done for output or internal signals). The
example indicates that only the positive transitions of signal \fIa\fP
are meaningful (and only the negative transitions of \fIc\fP).
For those signals, only toggle transitions may appear in the STG. 
When expansion to a 4-phase protocol is desired, the complementary signals
are inserted with maximum concurrency with the rest of transitions of
the STG in such a way that the encoding of the involved signal is maintained
consistent. In case that only a 2-phase protocol is desired, no
complementary transitions are inserted and the meaningful transitions are
considered as toggle transitions.
.sp
\fBChannels:\fP
.sp
\a.channels a b
.br
\a.internal_channels i1 i2
.br
\a.external_channels c
.sp
When expansion to a 2- or 4-phase protocol is desired, each channel is
implemented as 2 signals (1 input and 1 output). Only input (e.g. \fIa?\fP)
and output (e.g. \fIa!\fP) events are allowed for channels. For a 2-phase
protocol, input and output events are translated into toggle events of
the input and output signals respectively (\fIa? -> a_in~, a! -> a_out~\fP).
For a 4-phase protocol, input and output events are translated into rising
transitions of the input and output signals respectively. The return-to-zero
transitions are automatically inserted with maximum concurrency with the
rest of transitions.

Channels can also be declared with some polarity (positive or negative)
similarly to signals with free return-to-zero. For example:
.sp
\a.channels a+ b- c
.br
.sp
indicating that the meaningful transitions for signal \fIa\fP (\fIb\fP)
will be substituted by positive (negative) transitions. When no
polarity is specified (e.g. signal \fIc\fP), positive polarity is
assumed. As with signals with free return-to-zero, polarity is only
relevant when expansion to 4-phase protocol is required.

It is also possible to specify a different polarity for the input and
output signals. For example:
.sp
\a.channels a+- b-+
.br
.sp
The first sign corresponds to the input signal, whereas the second to
the output signal. Thus, the meaningful transitions of signal \fIa_in\fP
and \fIb_out\fP will be positive. On the other hand, the meaningful
transitions of \fIa_out\fP and \fIb_in\fP will be negative.

The declarations \fI.channels\fP and \fI.external channels\fP are
semantically equivalent. External channels are implemented with input and
output signals. Internal channels are implemented with only internal
signals.
.sp
\fBSignal values for the initial marking\fP (optional):
.sp
\a.initial state !a b
.sp
When the Petri net is interpreted as an STG, this option defines de
values of the signals in the initial marking of the net. This option is
useful when toggle transitions are used and the initial value of some
signal cannot be deduced from the flow analysis of the STG. When
necessary, \fBpetrify\fP assumes the value 0 for signals with undefined
initial value. In the example, the initial values
for signals \fIa\fP and \fIb\fP are defined to be 0 and 1 respectively.
Currently, the definition of the initial value for a signal is mandatory
when don't care (\fIa*\fP) or level (\fIa^0, a^1\fP) transitions are used
for that signal. It is also mandatory for signals supporting boolean guards.
This statement must go before the graph description.
.sp

.in -0.4i
The following declarations must go after the graph description:
.in +0.4i
.sp
\fBInitial marking\fP (mandatory):
.sp
\a.marking {p1 <a+,b+> p2=2 <c+,d->=3}
.sp
Initially \fIp2\fP has 2 tokens and \fI<c+,d->\fP 3 tokens (the rest
have 1 token)
.sp
\fBPlace capacity\fP:
.sp
\a.capacity  p2=3  <c+,d+>=4
.sp
Indicates the maximum capacity for each place (default is 1). This is intended
for boundedness check during token flow analysis.
.sp
\fBSlow events\fP:
.sp
\a.slow  a+/1 b? c!/2
.sp
Indicates that some events are assumed to be slow.
This declaration is used when concurrency is reduced (option \fB-redc\fP)
and when timing optimizations are applied (options \fB-topt\fP and 
\fB-atopt\fP).
The concurrency between pairs of slow signals is never reduced.
When solving CSC, slow events are allowed to be delayed by new state
signals under the assumptions that the internal delay of the circuit will
be always shorter than the delay of the environment with respect to the
events declared as slow. The declaration of slow events enables \fBpetrify\fP
to generate more aggressive relative timing assumptions between the
delays of the circuit and the environment.

.sp
\a.slowenv
.sp
It is a shorcut to declare all events of input signals as slow.
.sp
\fBRelative timing assumptions:\fP
.sp
\a.time a+/1 <| b-   x- > y+/2 > r-    x+=y+=z+@a-,b-  c+ <> d-
.sp
These are different declarations of relative timing constraints. The syntax
\fIa<|b\fP is used to declare that event \fIa\fP will fire before event 
\fIb\fP when both are concurrent. The syntax \fIa>b>c\fP is used to declare
that event \fIa\fP can be enabled earlier by some of its transitively
preceding trigger signals. For the list of signals to be valid \fIa\fP is
required to be triggered by \fIb\fP, \fIb\fP triggered by \fIc\fP, etc.
The syntax \fIx=y=z@a,b\fP indicates that the firing times of \fIx\fP, \fIy\fP
and \fIz\fP are considered to be non-distinguishable with respect to
the triggered events \fIa\fP and \fIb\fP (they must be triggered by some
of the events in the left part of the declaration). The syntax \fIa<>b\fP
indicates that no constraints of the type \fIa<|b\fP or \fIb<|a\fP 
(either specified or generated automatically) should be accepted for this
pair of events. The exact interpretation
of the relative timing assumptions is explained in more detail in the tutorial
of the tool.

\fBConstraints during concurrency reduction (.concurrent, .late 
and .ordered)\fP:
.sp
The following statements are only meaningful for the phase of concurrency
reduction (option \fB-redc\fP). The statements \fB.slow\fP
and \fB.slowenv\fP also impose constraints on this phase.
When channels or signals with free return-to-zero are specified in
4-phase expansion, only the active transitions of the signals are
affected by this statement.
.sp
\a.concurrent {<a+,b+> <c?,d!>}
.sp
Indicates that concurrency between the pairs of events must be preserved.
.sp
\a.late {<r+,s+> <a?,b!>}
.sp
Indicates that the pairs of specified events must be concurrent and,
in case their concurrency is reduced, they must be ordered as specified,
e.g. r+ before s+ and a? before b! (but not vice versa).
.sp
\a.ordered {<a-,b-> <x?,y!>}
.sp
Indicates that concurrency between the pairs of events is to be
reduced whenever possible (i.e. when the reduction does not affect
other events for which concurrency must be preserved).
.sp
\fBState graphs\fP:
.sp
\fBPetrify\fP also allows a new format to describe state graphs
in a more compact form. The keyword \fI.state graph\fP must be used instead
of \fI.graph\fP. The description of a state graph is as follows:
.sp
\a.state graph
.br
s0 a+ s1 b+ s2
.br
s2 a- s3
.br
s3 b- s0
.br
\a.marking {s0}
.sp
describes a state graph with four states. An arc with label \fIa+\fP goes
from \fIs0\fP to \fIs1\fP, an arc with label \fIb+\fP goes
from \fIs1\fP to \fIs2\fP, etc. The \fI.marking\fP statement is allowed
to specify only one state. Indexed transitions (e.g. \fIa+/2\fP) 
are not allowed in state graphs.
.SH EXAMPLES
\fBpetrify -o graph.out -hide .dummy -bisim graph.in\fP
.sp
generates a Petri net from the one described in \fIgraph.in\fP
and removes the dummy events preserving weak bisimilarity.
.sp
\fBpetrify -o graph.out -fc graph.in\fP
.sp
generates a free-choice Petri net from the one described in \fIgraph.in\fP.
.sp
\fBpetrify -o graph.out -csc graph.in\fP
.sp
generates an STG in which new state signals have been inserted
to force the CSC property.
.sp
\fBpetrify graph.in -gc -topt -eqn graph.eqn -log graph.log -no \fP
.sp
syntehsizes a circuit by solving CSC (if required), generating generalized
C elements and reporting information in the file \fIgraph.log\fP. The
equations of the circuit are reported in the file \fIgraph.eqn\fP.
Relative timing assumptions are taken into account to optimize the circuit.
.sp
\fBpetrify -o graph.out -eqn graph.eqn -er graph.in\fP
.sp
solves CSC, generates logic equations for a speed-independent circuit
using the default library for decomposition. The resulting STG (with the
new inserted signals) is written into \fIgraph.out\fP. In the new STG, each
disconnected excitation region has a different label (\fI-er\fP option).
Finally, the logic equations are written into the file \fIgraph.eqn\fP
in EQN format.
.sp
\fBpetrify -o graph.out -blif graph.blif -cg graph.in\fP
.sp
Similar as above, but generating a complex-gate implementation
(no decomposition is performed). The resulting circuit is dumped into
the file \fIgraph.blif\fP.
.sp
\fBpetrify -o graph.out -blif graph.blif -tm -lit3 -lib mylib.lib graph.in\fP
.sp
Logic decomposition is performed until all functions are mappable onto
library gates not exceeding 3 literals. After decomposition, technology
mapping is performed. The gate library is specified in the file
\fImylib.lib\fP.
.sp
\fBpetrify -o graph.out -blif graph.blif -tm -latch CD graph.in\fP
.sp
Logic decomposition is performed until all functions are mappable onto
library gates. Only C elements and D latches are used for decomposition
with latches.

